Tommi Syrjanen  <tommi.syrjanen@i.get.enough.spam.as.it.is.hut.fi> wrote:
>David Damerell <damerell@chiark.greenend.org.uk> writes:
>>The idea is not to make the average game harder up until the Castle. In
>>the average game, you don't _find_ an early WoW. The idea is to eliminate
>>the extreme outlying case, where the early and middle games are really
>>disproportionately easy.
>Do you also propose to eliminate the extreme outlying cases to the
>other direction? You know, the cases where the early and middle games
>are absolutely impossible, like the ones where you find the proverbial
>gnome with a wand of death?

That depends if those cases can be easily removed without changing the
non-outlier games, and without removing the interesting tactical
situations that the game's randomness creates. I don't think this is the
case here, because the gnome with the WoD is a very interesting situation
in the cases where it doesn't just kill you straight off.

I do agree that, where possible, the game should not kill you in a way
that even the best of players could not avoid. However, I think that
identifying those cases and removing them without altering the normal
gameplay is a much harder problem.

>>It is not bashing people to observe that ascension is much easier if you
>>get an early WoW. That's a plain, factual observation.
>And it is also a plain, factual observation that a character who finds
>speed boots and a bag of holding on dungeon level 2 has much easier
>time to ascend than one who doesn't.

Indeed. However, he has not had nearly so great a boost as the early WoW.
Obviously luck plays a part in the game; it _must_ play a part in the game
if it is to throw up the interestingly varied situations that make the
game replayable. That does not mean that outlying cases, where luck
becomes much more important than skill, should not be removed.

>As for limiting Wand of Wishing charges, I don't see a point for it.
>Finding an early one makes an ascension 90% certain? So what. If you
>find that such a break of luck makes the game uninteresting, then
>escape the dungeon and start a new one.

So why not give them 20 charges apiece?
